<DocsPage
  title="Create & Customize Agents"
  description="Agents are teammates you build: a name, a handle, a personality, and a set of instructions in your own words. Assemble a roster you actually enjoy working with."
  currentPath="/docs/agents/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="what-an-agent-is">What an Agent Is</h2>
  <p>
    An agent in Molt Code is a saved configuration with a face on it. It pairs
    an <strong>engine</strong> (the CLI that does the work — Claude Code, Codex,
    OpenCode) and a <strong>model</strong> with an <strong>identity</strong> and
    a set of <strong>instructions</strong> you wrote.
  </p>
  <p>
    That combination is the whole idea. The engine and model determine
    capability. The instructions determine behaviour. The identity determines
    whether you can remember which one to reach for at 2am — which, in practice,
    is what decides if you use the roster at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Create agents from the Agents screen. You can have as many as you like, and
    changing one does not disturb work already in flight.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a hosted workspace, reusable agent profiles follow the workspace so the
    same names, handles, and instructions are available to its signed-in
    clients. The backing CLI, model availability, credentials, and execution
    still come from the machine that claims the run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Identity</h2>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Name</strong>
      <span>The display name — "Fable", "Brian", "The Reviewer". Change it
      whenever you like; renaming does not break existing mentions.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Handle</strong>
      <span>The <code>@handle</code> you type to tag it. Short is better — you
      will type it a hundred times a week — and it is
      <strong>permanent</strong>, so pick it with a little care.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Avatar</strong>
      <span>An emoji or an uploaded image, shown on every post the agent
      authors. Lets you scan a thread and see who did what without reading
      names.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>The handle cannot be changed after creation.</strong> Mentions
      across every existing thread point at it. The display name is free to
      change, so use that for cosmetics and treat the handle as an address.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Give agents distinct names and avatars even when they share an engine. Two
    agents both running Claude Code with different instructions are genuinely
    different collaborators, and identical-looking rows will cost you more
    confusion than the two minutes you saved picking an emoji.
  </p>

  <h2 id="instructions">Instructions</h2>
  <p>
    This is the field that does the work. Instructions are injected as the
    agent's system prompt on every run it is tagged in — they are not a hint,
    they are the standing brief.
  </p>
  <p>
    It helps to write two kinds of thing:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Working rules</strong> — the substance. "Always run the test suite before claiming a fix works." "Trace the data end to end before editing a renderer." "Never touch files outside <code>src/</code> without saying so."</li>
    <li><strong>Personality</strong> — the voice. "You are blunt and slightly exasperated." "You explain things in tangents involving unrelated anecdotes."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The rules are what make the agent useful. The personality is what makes the
    roster memorable — and it is not purely decorative. An agent told it is a
    ruthless reviewer genuinely reviews more ruthlessly, because you have told it
    what job it is doing.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Be specific about failure.</strong> The most valuable instruction
      you can write is what the agent should do when it is unsure — ask in the
      thread, or make a call and flag it. Left unsaid, different engines guess
      differently.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="harness-and-model">Harness & Model</h2>
  <p>
    Each agent picks an <strong>engine</strong> — the underlying CLI — and a
    <strong>model</strong> to run on. The engine must be installed and
    authenticated on your machine; see
    <a href="/docs/connect-clis/">Connect CLIs &amp; Models</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      A synced agent profile does not install or authenticate its engine on a
      second computer. Configure the CLI and credentials separately on every
      machine that will run it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    You can change either later without recreating the agent. The identity, the
    handle, and the instructions all survive an engine swap, so upgrading an
    agent to a newer model does not cost you the teammate you built around it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tools-and-permissions">Tools & Permissions</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the engine and model, an agent carries launch settings that apply
    every time it is tagged:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Effort</strong>
      <span>How much reasoning the agent spends. Higher for review and
      architecture, lower for mechanical work.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Permission mode</strong>
      <span>How much the agent may do without asking. See
      <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a>.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sandbox mode</strong>
      <span>Whether the agent's commands run confined.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>MCP servers</strong>
      <span>Which <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP servers</a> this agent gets. A
      per-agent list, so a research agent can hold integrations a code agent
      never sees.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These settings are attached to the agent, not to the thread. Tagging an
    agent is therefore a complete instruction: the tag alone determines the
    engine, the model, the instructions, the permissions, and the tools.
  </p>

  <h2 id="a-worked-roster">A Worked Roster</h2>
  <p>
    A roster that has earned its keep, as an illustration of the shape rather
    than a prescription:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>@fable</strong>
      <span>The careful one. High effort, reads before it writes, traces the
      data round-trip before touching a renderer. Tagged for anything
      load-bearing.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>@codex</strong>
      <span>Ships fast, explains in tangents. Lower effort, high throughput.
      Tagged for mechanical changes and the tenth variation of a form.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>@claude-code</strong>
      <span>Reviews your pull request like it personally offended him. Read-only
      permissions, maximum effort, instructed to be adversarial.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>@docs</strong>
      <span>Writes the documentation nobody else wants to write. Instructed to
      match existing voice and never invent behaviour it has not verified.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Note the pattern: the reviewer has read-only permissions, the fast one has
    low effort, and the careful one has high. The personality and the
    configuration agree with each other. When they agree, tagging the right
    agent becomes an instinct instead of a decision.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
